In the Standard Model of particle physics, electroweak symmetry breaking is implemented through a complex scalar SU(2)_L doublet H with potential V(H) = -mu^2 H^\dagger H + lambda (H^\dagger H)^2, where mu^2 > 0 and lambda > 0. After spontaneous symmetry breaking, the physical Higgs boson acquires a mass m_H = sqrt(2) mu, measured experimentally to be approximately 125 GeV. The parameter mu^2 is taken as a free input of the Standard Model, and its value is technically unnatural in the sense that radiative corrections from any heavier scale Lambda generically shift mu^2 by an amount of order Lambda^2, so that obtaining m_H of electroweak size in the presence of a much higher fundamental scale (such as the Planck scale M_Pl ~ 10^19 GeV) requires extreme fine-tuning.

Problem: Provide a fundamental theoretical mechanism, embedded in a consistent extension of the Standard Model of particle physics and of the standard cosmological model, that explains the physical origin of the Higgs boson mass parameter mu^2 (equivalently, m_H ~ 125 GeV). The mechanism must:
(a) derive the observed value of m_H from more basic principles or parameters rather than postulating it;
(b) be technically natural, i.e. stable against radiative corrections from any heavier physical scales present in the theory;
(c) be consistent with all current experimental constraints from collider physics, precision electroweak measurements, and cosmology.

